  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q30-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "30",
    "prompt": "A pilot flew at a constant speed for 600 km to its destination. Returning the next day, the pilot flew against a headwind of 50 km/h to return to the starting point. If the plane was in the air for a total of 7 hours, what was the speed of this plane on its outbound flight?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$200\\text{ km/h}$. Use $\\dfrac{600}{s}+\\dfrac{600}{s-50}=7$ and reject $s=\\dfrac{150}{7}$ because $s>50$.",
      "worked": "Let $s$ be the outbound speed in km/h. The return speed is $(s-50)$ km/h, so $s>50$. Using $t=\\dfrac{d}{s}$:\n$$t_{\\text{outbound}}=\\frac{600}{s},\\qquad t_{\\text{return}}=\\frac{600}{s-50}.$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{600}{s}+\\frac{600}{s-50}&=7\\\\600(s-50)+600s&=7s(s-50)\\\\1200s-30000&=7s^2-350s\\\\7s^2-1550s+30000&=0\\\\7s^2-1400s-150s+30000&=0\\\\7s(s-200)-150(s-200)&=0\\\\(7s-150)(s-200)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $s=\\dfrac{150}{7}$ or $s=200$. Reject $\\dfrac{150}{7}$ because it gives a negative return speed. The outbound speed is $200\\text{ km/h}$.\nCheck: $\\dfrac{600}{200}+\\dfrac{600}{150}=3+4=7$ hours.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
